  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nThis Afternoon to Thursday Morning: Dry and Pleasant Conditions\n\nA cooler, drier airmass will remain in place through the rest of \ntoday into Thursday morning over the Upper Midwest. Pleasant \nconditions are forecast during this time frame with light winds\nand mostly clear skies. Light winds and clear skies tonight may\nsupport the formation of some patchy fog, especially in the \nriver valleys of far southwestern Wisconsin. Otherwise, Thursday\nwill be a few degrees warmer with increasing cloud cover out \nahead of the next weather system.\n\nThursday Afternoon through Friday: Thunderstorm Potential\n\nA shortwave trough, originating out of the Canadian Prairie \nProvinces, is forecast to move southeast towards the Great Lakes \nregion Thursday into Friday. Out ahead of this system, mid level \nmoisture will increase which will result in the formation of showers \nand thunderstorms from the Northern Plains down through the Upper \nMidwest. Although it is possible a storm may be strong to marginally \nsevere as they move into the western CWA on Thursday evening, \nconfidence in that scenario is low since CIN is rapidly increasing \nas storms approach the area. Flooding is not really a concern with \nthe storms either, as PWAT values are only near the 75th percentile \n(based on sounding climatology from KMPX) and the storms should be \nmoving relatively quickly. The bulk of the precipitation should be \neast of the area by sunrise on Friday. Cloud cover should clear out \nthroughout the day as drier air makes its way over the area once \nagain and temperatures should be able to rise up into the upper 70s \nto lower 80s during the afternoon.\n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Seasonal and Mostly Dry Conditions\n\nForecast guidance remains consistent in building a ridge of high \npressure across the western and central US with a trough across the \neastern US this weekend into the next work week. This pattern would \nplace the Upper Mississippi River Valley in between these systems, \nwhich does lead to uncertainty regarding the exact details of the \nforecast throughout this time frame. In general, this pattern favors \nseasonal temperatures and mostly dry conditions. Ensemble forecast \nguidance hints at the potential for some light rainfall early next \nweek, however that will highly depend upon moisture remaining over \nthe area as well as the precise track of upper level disturbances.",
